s allowed and means no bases.\nR2. The result is built by starting with an empty object, merging each base file's result in the order listed (a later one overrides an earlier one), and then merging the file's own keys on top. A base file's own `extends` is resolved the same way, so the process is recursive. The `extends` key itself is not part of the result.\nR3. Merging: when both sides of a key are plain objects (not arrays, not `null`) they are merged key by key with the same rules; in every other case the later value replaces the earlier one completely. Arrays are replaced, not joined, and a later `null` replaces an earlier value.\nR4. A file that, directly or through its bases, extends itself makes `loadConfig` throw a `RangeError` whose message contains `cycle` and the chain of normalized paths joined with ` -> `, ending with the path that closed the loop (for example `cycle: /a.json -> /b.json -> /a.json`). Reaching the same file twice by different routes (a diamond) is not a cycle.\nR5. A file whose text is not valid JSON throws a `SyntaxError` whose message contains that file's path. A file whose JSON is not an object (an array, a string, `null`, and so on) throws a `TypeError` whose message contains its path, as before. This applies to base files too. An error thrown by `readFile` is passed on unchanged.\nR6. Each file is read at most once in one call to `loadConfig`, even if several files extend it.\nR7. A key named `__proto__` in a JSON file is an ordinary key: it is kept as an own property of the result, at any depth, and is merged like any other key (R3). Loading never changes `Object.prototype` or any other prototype, and the result and every object inside it still have `Object.prototype` as their prototype.\n\nWhen all the rules hold, reply with the single word: done"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"reasoning_content":"Let's start by checking the existing files.\n","tool_calls":[{"id":"HQu12e8K0PlHwIaLsN0hDX8zOEZNNn8H","type":"function","funct